I’ll first inventory the measurement, chemical constraints, helper scripts, and candidate databases. Then I’ll health-screen plausible structures, fit competing phase inventories in GSAS-II, and only retain phases whose scales and diffraction families remain stable through replay and profile challenges.

The chemistry points first to Mg–W–O products, but the precursor names alone do not provide a fixed molar ratio. I’m treating MgWO₄, residual MgO/WO₃, and charge-balanced non-1:1 magnesium tungstates as the initial watch list; I’ll distinguish records/polymorphs by their actual peak families rather than formula alone.

The first screen already shows the dominant wolframite-type MgWO₄ lattice: its strongest calculated lines track the observed 15.58°, 18.88°, 23.82°, 30.41/30.73°, and high-angle families. A second, broad set near 23.1–24.4°, 33–34°, and 41–42° is consistent with monoclinic WO₃, so I’m finishing the polymorph comparison before refining either fraction.

I’m now moving from line matching to full-profile source competition. Each WO₃ record will start from the same neutral checkpoint (large crystallite size, zero strain, March ratio 1, zero roughness), with identical background, scale, cell, zero-shift, and common U/W/X treatment; this prevents a favorable profile nuisance term from deciding the polymorph ranking.

The exact-formula source ranking is decisive at the common checkpoint: COD 2311041 (monoclinic P2₁/c WO₃) gives \(R_{wp}=8.584\%\), versus 8.962% for the alternate monoclinic record, 10.719% for triclinic, 11.640% for orthorhombic, and 15.441% for tetragonal WO₃. I’m retaining 2311041 and now testing phase-specific size/strain from that same checkpoint; MgO remains provisional until its scale survives those profile challenges and a phase-removal replay.

The broadening loop rejects MgWO₄ and MgO phase-width terms: size runs hit the 10 μm neutral ceiling with singular uncertainty, while their strain runs went negative. WO₃ alone supports positive broadening (about 0.088 μm size or \(6.97\times10^3\) microstrain), with fractions stable near 41.1% WO₃ and 7.5% MgO. Its hkl families do have unequal widths, so I’m challenging the isotropic result with low-index uniaxial and symmetry-compatible Stephens models, then replaying the serious WO₃ source competitors with the same elaboration.

A clear, replay-stable intensity effect emerged: MgWO₄ March–Dollase texture along [010] refines to 1.3000 and lowers \(R_{wp}\) from 7.062% to 2.293%, while the phase fractions remain essentially unchanged (51.35/41.11/7.54 wt%). Competing axes are much worse, so this is not a generic scale/background surrogate. I’m accepting [010] provisionally and restarting the loop from that checkpoint to test WO₃/MgO texture, background order, polarization, and broadening again.

The phase inventory has converged to MgWO₄ + monoclinic WO₃ + MgO. I’m finishing the nuisance-term replay, then I’ll challenge the weak MgO phase and alternate WO₃ sources against the same refined checkpoint before freezing and exporting the full-range project.

The second replay supports a modest 8-term smooth background; roughness is singular/highly correlated and is rejected. Polarization is statistically nonzero in the simpler-background trial, so I’m replaying it with the selected background before deciding whether that angular intensity correction is real or compensatory.

The weak-phase challenge is decisive: removing MgO leaves strong unmatched intensity and degrades Rwp from about 2.25% to 7.30%, even after allowing WO₃ maximal anisotropic broadening. The selected WO₃ record also remains far ahead of the next structural competitors after the MgWO₄ texture correction (about 2.3% versus 6.6% and 9.0% Rwp).

Both texture terms remain replay-stable with the accepted angular correction: MgWO₄ [010] is the dominant intensity correction, while WO₃ shows a much smaller [001] trend. I’m now rerunning the full WO₃ width-model comparison from that combined checkpoint; this is the last topology loop before the full-range freeze.

The final retained model uses a 9-term smooth background, refined polarization, MgWO₄ [010] and weak WO₃ [001] March–Dollase terms, and generalized WO₃ microstrain; the extra background, roughness, crystallite-size, and MgWO₄/MgO strain terms were unsupported or unstable. I’m replaying this pruned model over the exact measured 9.903–100.095° range and exporting directly from that reopened project.

The full-range replay is converged at Rwp 2.2266%, with fractions 51.37% MgWO₄, 41.15% WO₃, and 7.48% MgO. All three exported CIFs pass the geometry/occupancy health screen; I’m doing the final cross-file consistency check now so the JSON, CIF paths, profile, fractions, and GPX all refer to exactly the same saved refinement.

Completed. The modeled crystalline fractions are:

- MgWO₄ (P2/c): 51.37 wt%
- Monoclinic WO₃ (P2₁/c): 41.15 wt%
- MgO (Fm-3m): 7.48 wt%

Full-range fit: Rwp = 2.2266%, Rp = 1.6990%, GOF = 1.4613. All CIFs passed the final health screen, and cross-file consistency checks passed. Fractions exclude any amorphous material; literature atomic coordinates were retained.

